Fig. 6. Selected measurements of the extraterrestrial electron spectrum in the range 2 Mev to 3 bev. Solid line represents the measurements of Rockstroh and Webber [1969] in 1965, open diamonds in 1966, and solid diamonds in 1968. Measurements by Simnett and McDonald [1969] at low energies are shown as a dotted curve, and those of Beuermann et al. [1969] are shown as solid squares. diffusion-convection model. We have $$
M = \frac{\eta(t_2)}{K_2(P, t_2)} - \frac{\eta(t_1)}{K_2(P, t_1)}
$$ and, if the form of $K_2(P, t)$ is the same at times $t_1$ and $t_2$, then $M = [\Delta\eta]/[K_2(P)]$, where $\Delta\eta = \eta(t_2) - \eta(t_1)$. The rigidity dependence of the diffusion coefficient has been related to the power spectrum of the interplanetary magnetic field inhomogeneities [Roelof, 1966; Jokipii, 1966]. For example, if the frequency power spectrum of magnetic field inhomogeneities is given by $$
\frac{dQ}{df} \sim \frac{1}{f^p}
$$ ($dQ/df$ is the power $dQ$ per frequency interval $df$ at the frequency $f$) over a certain frequency range, then the rigidity dependence of the dif- fusion coefficient is given by $K_2(P, t) = P^{2-p}$ over the corresponding rigidity range. The rigidity corresponding to a given frequency can be obtained by assuming that particles of rigidity $P$ are most effectively scattered by magnetic inhomogeneities of wave number $k = (1/\rho)$, where $\rho$ is the particle's gyroradius in the interplanetary magnetic field $B$. (This assumption is shown to be valid by Roelof [1966] and Jokipii [1966]). The gyroradius $\rho$ is given by $\rho = P/300B$, where $\rho$ is the gyroradius in cm, $P$ is the particle rigidity in volts, and $B$ is the magnitude of the interplanetary magnetic field in gauss. For a magnetic field detection instrument stationary with respect to the solar wind, inhomogeneities of wavelength $\lambda$ will produce a frequency component $f = V/\lambda$, where $V$ is the velocity of the solar wind in which the magnetic inhomogeneities are convected. Since $k = (2\pi/\lambda)$ and since we take <!-- page 9 -->
$k = (1/\rho)$, we have $f = (300VB)/(2\pi\rho)$, where the units of $P$ and $B$ are as given above and $V$ is given in cm/sec. By taking $V = 400$ km/sec, $B = 5 \times 10^{-5}$ gauss and by adopting units of bv for $P$ we have $$
f(\text{Hz}) = \frac{0.95 \times 10^{-4}}{P(\text{bv})}
$$ The simple diffusion-convection theory predicts that the modulation parameter $M$ depends on the particle only through its rigidity and thus will be the same for protons, helium nuclei, and electrons at the same rigidity, regardless of the form of $K_2(P, t)$ and its time dependence. In other words, no charge splitting of the modulation parameter should be observed. Hsieh [1970] recently noted that one should be careful to use the appropriate values of $p$ when the data from two time periods are compared. In particular, $$
M = \frac{\eta(t_2)}{P^{2-p(t_2)}} - \frac{\eta(t_1)}{P^{2-p(t_1)}}
$$ if $p(t_1) \neq p(t_2)$. The above expression for $$
M \equiv \beta \ln \frac{j(r, t_1, T)}{j(r, t_2, T)}
$$ under the condition $p(t_1) \neq p(t_2)$ was used by Hsieh [1970] in a comparison of proton and helium data obtained in 1967 from Imp 4 ($j(r, t_2, T)$) and in 1965 from Imp 3 ($j(r, t_1, T)$). This analysis revealed that the modulation parameter decreased with decreasing rigidity at low rigidities for both protons and helium nuclei, in contrast to the well-established behavior at higher rigidities, where the modulation parameter is inversely proportional to rigidity [Lockwood and Webber, 1967b]. The low-energy data presented by Hsieh [1970] for protons and helium nuclei were obtained over a restricted rigidity interval, and it was therefore possible to fit the same expression $$
\frac{\eta(t_2)}{P^{2-p(t_2)}} - \frac{\eta(t_1)}{P^{2-p(t_1)}}
$$ to both proton and helium modulation parameter data with reasonable choices of $\eta(t_1)$, $\eta(t_2)$, and $p(t_1) \neq p(t_2)$. A charge splitting was not evident from these data, since proton and helium nuclei modulations were not ob- tained in an overlapping rigidity range. We will see that this is not the case for the Pioneer 8 proton and helium nuclei data. We have determined the modulation parameter $$
M \equiv \beta \ln \left[ \frac{j(r, t_1, T)}{j(r, t_2, T)} \right]
$$ by using quiettime 1968 proton and helium nuclei data obtained from Pioneer 8 ($j(r, t_2, T)$) and data obtained near solar minimum ($j(r, t_1, T)$). The modulation parameter as a function of rigidity is presented in Figure 7. Separate smooth curves are passed through the data points for protons and helium nuclei. Curves that best fit the individual proton and helium nuclei data presented by Hsieh [1970] are shown as two dashed lines. The dotted line shows the modulation function that Hsieh devised to fit these data by using the diffusion-convection theory with $p(t_1) \neq p(t_2)$. From the Pioneer data we see that at low rigidities a decrease in the modulation parameter with decreasing rigidity is apparent, just as it was from data obtained from Imp 4. However, the Pioneer 8 data are obtained over a larger rigidity range than data from the Imp 4 experiment, and, furthermore, the data for protons and helium nuclei are obtained in an overlapping rigidity interval. This implies some interesting conclusions. First, above $\sim 1$ bv the modulation parameter as a function of rigidity is similar for protons and helium nuclei. Below $\lesssim 1$ bv the modulation parameter is larger for protons than for helium nuclei. Thus a 'splitting' is seen in the modulation parameter as a function of rigidity for protons and helium nuclei. Second, a maximum occurs in the magnitude of the modulation parameter for both protons and helium nuclei. The rigidity at which the modulation parameter reaches a maximum can be estimated as $0.65 \pm 0.05$ bv for protons and $1.0 \pm 0.1$ bv for helium nuclei. The splitting of the modulation parameter as a function of rigidity for protons and helium nuclei is inconsistent with the simple diffusion-convection model for the modulation of galactic cosmic rays. This model predicts the same value of the modulation parameter for protons and helium nuclei at the same rigidity as discussed above. The fact that it is possible that <!-- page 10 -->
Fig. 7. The modulation parameter $M \equiv \beta \ln [j_{1965}/j]$ for the Pioneer 8 proton and helium data ($j = j_{1968}$) is presented as a function of rigidity. Open circles indicate protons and solid circles indicate helium nuclei. Solid curves have been drawn in to fit these data. Dashed curves represent the best fit to the individual proton and helium nuclei modulation parameter data from Imp 4 by Hsieh [1970]. The dotted line shows the modulation function devised by Hsieh [1970] to fit his data by using the diffusion-convection theory with $p(t_1) \neq p(t_2)$. $p(t_1) \neq p(t_2)$, as suggested by Hsieh [1970], does not alter this prediction. It thus follows that a modification of the simple diffusion-convection model is necessary to explain the observations. To obtain a more satisfactory explanation of the observations than is given by simple diffusion-convection theory, we have obtained solutions of the modulation equations that include the effects of adiabatic energy change. The first approach we considered along these lines was to use the 'force-field' solution of Gleeson and Axford [1968a]. The transport equation presented above can be written as follows: $$
\frac{\partial U}{\partial t} + \frac{1}{r^2} \frac{\partial}{\partial r} (r^2 S) = -\frac{V}{3} \frac{\partial^2}{\partial T \partial r} (\alpha T U)
$$ where the radial current density $S(r, t, T)$ is given by $$
\begin{aligned} S &= VU - K \frac{\partial U}{\partial r} - \frac{V}{3} \frac{\partial}{\partial T} (\alpha T U) \\ &= VU \left(1 - \frac{1}{3U} \frac{\partial}{\partial T} (\alpha T U)\right) - K \frac{\partial U}{\partial r} \end{aligned}
$$ The corresponding expression from diffusion-convection theory is given by $S = VU - K(\partial U/\partial r)$ with the term $-V/3(\partial/\partial T)(\alpha T U)$ absent. This term appears in the present ex- pression for the radial current density because of the Compton-Getting effect on the convection flux $VU$. The Compton-Getting factor $1 - (1/3U)(\partial/\partial T)(\alpha T U)$ represents the effect of transforming from a frame of reference stationary with respect to the solar wind to one stationary with respect to the earth [Gleeson and Axford, 1968b]. At energies greater than a few hundred Mev/nuc one can set the radial current density equal to zero [Gleeson and Axford, 1968a].

# COSMIC RAYS IN THE INTERPLANETARY MEDIUM L. J. GLEESON AND W. I. AXFORD\* Cornell–Sydney University Astronomy Center, Cornell University, Ithaca, New York Received June 1, 1967 In this Letter we discuss the equations governing the behavior of cosmic-ray particles in a model of the interplanetary medium. The equations provide direct information concerning the streaming and spatial gradient of cosmic rays and when combined reduce to the Fokker-Planck equation used in this context by Parker (1965, 1966). It is shown that in suitable conditions the cosmic rays behave as if they were being modulated by a heliocentric electric field, although this can only be an approximate description of the over-all modulation. We consider a spherically symmetric configuration in which the solar wind carries magnetic irregularities radially with speed $V(\boldsymbol{r})$ . The magnetic irregularities are assumed to act as perfectly elastic scatterers of cosmic rays in a frame of reference moving with the solar wind, and to have a number density $N(\boldsymbol{r})$ and scattering cross-section $\sigma(v)$ , where v is the velocity of a cosmic-ray particle. The configuration can include a radial magnetic field, but this does not affect the results. The Boltzmann equation appropriate to this situation is $$
\left[ \frac {\partial}{\partial t} + v \cos \Theta \frac {\partial}{\partial r} - \frac {v}{r} \sin \Theta \frac {\partial}{\partial \Theta} \right] f (r, v, \Theta , t) = \left(\frac {\delta f}{\delta t}\right) _ {c}, \tag {1}
$$ where $\Theta$ is the angle between v and r and $(\delta f/\delta t)_{c}$ represents the effects of scattering. We evaluate $(\delta f/\delta t)_{c}$ first in the frame which moves with the scatterers, and make a relativistic transformation of the result back to the fixed frame (L. J. Gleeson, in preparation). Thus, for isotropic scattering $$
\left(\frac {\delta f}{\delta t}\right) _ {c} = - 4 \pi N (r) \sigma (v _ {m}) v _ {m} \left(1 - \frac {V v \cos \Theta}{c ^ {2}}\right) f (r, v, \Theta , t)
$$ $$
+ \frac {2 \pi N (r) v _ {m}}{(1 - V v \cos \Theta / c ^ {2}) ^ {4}} \int_ {0} ^ {\pi} \sigma (v _ {m}) \left(1 - \frac {v ^ {\prime} V \cos \Theta^ {\prime}}{c ^ {2}}\right) ^ {5} f (r, v ^ {\prime}, \Theta^ {\prime}, t) \sin \Theta_ {m} ^ {\prime} d \Theta_ {m} ^ {\prime},
$$ where the subscript $m$ refers to the moving frame and primes to the particle velocity before scattering. The first and second terms in equation (2) represent scattering out of and into a given element of phase space, respectively. It is assumed that the distribution function can be written as a Legendre expansion as follows: $$
f (r, v, \Theta , t) = \sum_ {i = 0} ^ {\infty} f _ {i} (r, v, t) P _ {i} (\cos \Theta). \tag {3}
$$ We substitute this into equations (1) and (2), make a Taylor expansion of $(\delta f/\delta t)_{c}$ in powers of $(V/v)$ , and assume near-isotropy so that $f_{i} \ll f_{0}, f_{1}(i \geq 2)$ . Note that $V/v \approx 10^{-2}$ for 10 MeV protons, and that the observed deviations from isotropy are small \* Present address: Departments of Physics and Applied Electrophysics, University of California, San Diego, La Jolla, California. (McCracken 1966). Retaining terms necessary to determine $f_0$ and $f_1$ to $O(Vf / v)$ then, from the coefficients of $P_0$ and $P_1$ , we obtain $$
\begin{array}{l} 3 \frac {\partial f _ {0}}{\partial t} + \frac {1}{r ^ {2}} \frac {\partial}{\partial r} (r ^ {2} v f _ {1}) = \frac {4 \pi N V}{v ^ {2}} \frac {\partial}{\partial v} [ \sigma v ^ {3} (1 - v ^ {2} / c ^ {2}) f _ {1} ] (4) \\ \left. + \frac {4 \pi N V ^ {2}}{v ^ {2}} \frac {\partial}{\partial v} \left\{\frac {\sigma v ^ {3}}{\left(1 - v ^ {2} / c ^ {2}\right) ^ {1 / 2}} \frac {\partial}{\partial v} \left[ \left(1 - \frac {v ^ {2}}{c ^ {2}}\right) ^ {5 / 2} f _ {0} \right] \right\}, \right. \\ \frac {\partial f _ {1}}{\partial t} + v \frac {\partial f _ {0}}{\partial r} = - 4 \pi N \sigma v f _ {1} - \frac {4 \pi N \sigma V v}{(1 - v ^ {2} / c ^ {2}) ^ {3 / 2}} \frac {\partial}{\partial v} [ (1 - v ^ {2} / c ^ {2}) ^ {5 / 2} f _ {0} ]. (5) \\ \end{array}
$$ It has been assumed that the distribution function is sufficiently smooth for $f$ , $v\partial f / \partial v$ , and $v^{2}\partial^{2}f / \partial v^{2}$ to be considered of the same order of magnitude. Care must be taken (especially in the coefficient of $P_0$ ) to insure that all significant terms are included. Since $\partial f / \partial r \sim (4\pi N\sigma V / v)f$ and $f_{1}$ is roughly of order $(Vf_0 / v)$ , it is necessary to include terms involving $V^{2}f_{0}$ in equation (4); the terms which have been neglected are of order $(V^{3}f_{0} / v^{3})$ in equation (4) and $(V^{2}f_{0} / v^{2})$ in equation (5). In general $$
\frac {\partial f _ {1}}{\partial t} \ll 4 \pi N \sigma v f _ {1} = f _ {1} \times (\text {collision frequency}), \tag {6}
$$ and hence $\partial f_{1}/\partial t$ (which represents the effects of inertia of the cosmic-ray gas) can usually be neglected in equation (5) (cf. Axford 1965). For the case of galactic cosmic rays, changes with time usually take place relatively slowly so that conditions can be considered to be quasi-steady and $\partial f_{0}/\partial t$ in equation (4) can also be neglected. The effects associated with the spherical symmetry are represented entirely by the term $1 / r^2 (\partial /\partial r)(r^2 vf_1)$ on the left-hand side of equation (4). If this is replaced by $1 / r^{\nu}$ $(\partial /\partial r)(r^{\nu}vf_{1})$ the equations become appropriate to the cases of plane, cylindrical, or spherical geometry according as $\nu = 0,1,$ or 2, respectively. The quantities $f_{0}(r,v,t)$ and $f_{1}(r,v,t)$ are related to the total number density $n(r,t)$ and bulk radial speed $u(r,t)$ through $$
n = 4 \pi \int_ {0} ^ {c} v ^ {2} f _ {0} d v, \quad n u = \frac {4 \pi}{3} \int_ {0} ^ {c} v ^ {3} f _ {1} d v. \tag {7}
$$ The continuity equation for the cosmic-ray gas $$
\frac {\partial n}{\partial t} + \frac {1}{r ^ {\nu}} \frac {\partial}{\partial r} (r ^ {\nu} n u) = 0 \tag {8}
$$ is obtained on integrating equation (4) with respect to v, while equation (5) is similarly related to the radial momentum equation. The number density $U(\boldsymbol{r}, T, t)$ and radial current density $S(\boldsymbol{r}, T, t)$ per unit interval of kinetic energy (T) are given by $$
U d T = 4 \pi v ^ {2} f _ {0} d v, \quad S d T = \frac {4 \pi}{3} v ^ {3} f _ {1} d v. \tag {9}
$$ Similarly the mean differential intensity $j_{0}(r,T,t)$ and the differential anisotropy $\xi(r,T,t)$ are given by $$
j _ {0} = \frac {1}{4 \pi} v U, \quad \xi = \frac {f _ {1}}{f _ {0}} = \frac {3 S}{v U}. \tag {10}
$$ In terms of $U, S$ , and $T$ , and neglecting $\partial f_1 / \partial t$ , equations (4) and (5) become $$
\frac {\partial U}{\partial t} + \frac {1}{r ^ {\nu}} \frac {\partial}{\partial r} (r ^ {\prime} S) = \frac {V}{3} \frac {\partial}{\partial T} \left\{\frac {a T}{\kappa} \left[ S - V U + \frac {V}{3} \frac {\partial}{\partial T} (a T U) \right] \right\}, \tag {11}
$$ $$
\kappa \frac {\partial U}{\partial r} = - S + V U - \frac {V}{3} \frac {\partial}{\partial T} (a T U), \tag {12}
$$ where $\kappa = \frac{1}{3} v / (4\pi N\sigma)$ is the diffusion coefficient and, with $m$ the particle mass,

with $r_0 = 0.511$ MV. Figure 3 shows the analytic approximations in comparison with numerical results (crosses) for small, medium and high rigidities. For both models (DT and RS) we have very good agreement between the approximations and the numerical results for the parallel mean free path. **Fig. 5.** The parallel spatial diffusion coefficient $\kappa_{\parallel}$ for the random sweeping model. ### 5.3. The parallel spatial diffusion coefficient In this section we calculate the parallel spatial diffusion coefficient $\kappa_{\parallel}$. With the equations for the mean free path and $$
\kappa_{\parallel} = \frac{v}{3} \lambda \tag{55}
$$ we can write the parallel spatial diffusion coefficient as $$
\frac{\kappa_{\parallel}}{\kappa_0} = \frac{r}{\sqrt{r_0^2 + r^2}} \frac{\lambda}{\lambda_0} \tag{56}
$$ with $$
\kappa_0 = \frac{c\lambda_0}{3}. \tag{57}
$$ Figures 4 and 5 show $\kappa_{\parallel}/\kappa_0$ for the DT- and the RS-model, respectively. If we would adopt as in many previous studies the simple relation $\kappa_{\perp} = \alpha\kappa_{\parallel}$ with constant $\alpha$, Figs. 4 and 5 also show the rigidity dependence of the perpendicular spatial diffusion coefficient for the dynamical turbulence and random sweeping turbulence model. ## 6. The mean free path for different values of the dissipation range spectral index $p$ In the last section we calculated the mean free path for special heliospheric parameters. Because of the limited time resolution of plasma detectors in spacecraft measurements, it is not clear that the dissipation range spectral index $p$ is equal to 3. In this chapter we therefore calculate the mean free path for different values of the dissipative spectral index $p$. We restrict our analysis to particle rigidities smaller than $10^4$ MV, because we know <!-- page 9 -->
**Fig. 6.** Parallel mean free paths for electrons, positrons and protons in the damping model for different values of the dissipation range spectral index $p$. **Fig. 7.** Parallel mean free paths for electrons, positrons and protons in the random sweeping model for different values of the dissipation range spectral index $p$. that for higher rigidities the mean free paths for both damping models (DT and RS) are independent of $p$ (see Eq. (34)). With Table 3 for the DT-model and Table 4 for the RS-model it is straightforward to calculate the mean free path for different dissipation range spectral indices $p$. The results are shown in Fig. 6 for the DT-model and Fig. 7 for the RS-model. For both figures we used the parameters of Eq. (50). We notice that there is no $p$-dependence for protons in both models. For the DT-model and for electrons and positrons we only find small differences for medium rigidities. For higher rigidities there is no $p$-dependence and for small rigidities in agreement with our analysis in Sect. 4 we find that the mean free path is proportional to $(p-2)/(p-s)$. For the RS-model we find that the dependence of $p$ is very strong. For higher values of $p$ and for medium rigidities we find that the value of the mean free path becomes larger than the size of the heliosphere. The fact that the parallel mean free path becomes larger than the dimensions of the heliosphere, may actually not be a problem. Because the magnitude of the perpendicular mean free path is typically only a few percent of the parallel mean free path, the effective radial diffusion coefficient may still be small enough to justify a diffusion approximation. ## 7. Summary and conclusion The parallel mean free path of cosmic ray particles in partially turbulent electromagnetic fields is a key input parameter for cosmic ray transport. In this work we have calculated the parallel mean free path of cosmic ray particles in two particular turbulence models: slab-like dynamical and random sweeping turbulence. We extended our earlier calculations by allowing a flat energy range in the power spectrum with finite wave amplitudes at very small wavenumbers. We derive new general formulas for the mean free path (see Tables 3 and 4). These new formulas can also be used for high particle rigidities. We explicitly determine the rigidity dependence and the absolute value of the mean free path for different cosmic ray particles for the DT- and RS-model. In both models the non-zero plasma wave intensity at wavenumbers smaller than $k_{min}$ yields smaller values of the mean free path at high rigidities as compared to the power spectrum with no wave intensity below $k_{min}$ considered in TS. We also discuss the influence of the dissipation range spectral index $p$ and the asymptotic properties of the mean free path for both models. ## Appendix A: Solving the integral $D$ for the damping model of dynamical turbulence For the damping model of dynamical turbulence we must solve the integral $$
D = \int_{0}^{\infty} \frac{dx}{x} \left[ \frac{1}{1 + a^2/R^2 (\mu R - x)^2} + \frac{1}{1 + a^2/R^2 (\mu R + x)^2} \right]. \quad (A.1)
$$ This integral can be solved exactly and we find that $$
