Thus, an additional modulation effect is suggested, involving an outer barrier or a region where equation (2) does not apply. Although Axford (1985) finds that $r_{b}=100$ AU as the likely pressure balance point between the internal and external heliospheric plasma and particles, Webber (1987) argues that $r_{b}\sim50$ AU is more reasonable. While the 3% AU $^{-1}$ gradient extrapolates the solar minum cosmic-ray intensity to the estimated interstellar value at about 50 AU, this same gradient at solar maximum would require $r_{b}$ to be much greater. The interpolation of a variable-strength additional barrier modulation at $50\lesssim r\lesssim100$ AU would overcome this difficulty. Two physical effects operating in current nonbarrier modulation models simulate the effects of a barrier but do not explain the data. The first concerns an axisymmetric no-drift model, where diffusion perpendicular to the field takes over from diffusion parallel to the field as the Archimedes spiral angle approaches $90^{\circ}$ . Since this occurs within 20 AU, the consequent intensity gradient increase is too close to simulate the required barrier (Potgieter and LeRoux 1987a). The second involves the axisymmetric drift model where flow from the poles at A = +1 or along the neutral sheet at A = -1 dominates the effect of particle diffusion (Jokipii and Thomas 1981). At A = +1, a steep gradient appears at the model boundary (Jokipii and Kopriva 1979) which is clearly due to a switch from drift in from polar field lines to direct cross-field diffusion from the boundary. However, this effect is not present at A = -1 and so cannot be the required barrier. A final point must be considered, even where we accept the need for a barrier somewhere beyond 50 AU, operating during both the $A = +1$ and $A = -1$ periods. This is: Can the additional modulation existing inside the barrier be understood in the context of current theory? Potgieter et al. (1987b) explored the drift model with tilted neutral sheet (see Jokipii and Thomas 1981) and the further effect of artificially reducing the latitude drift. With a tilt angle of $30^{\circ}$ and full latitude drift, the radial gradients in 1980–1990 ( $A = -1$ ) are larger than in 1970–1980 ( $A = +1$ ), contrary to observation, and are also too small in absolute terms in 1970–1980. Drift velocity reduction by a factor of 10 allowed both an increase of gradients for $A = +1$ to about $2\% \mathrm{AU}^{-1}$ and even made the $A = -1$ gradient to be less than the $A = +1$ gradient beyond 30 AU. This still fails quantitatively to explain the IMP 8-Voyager 2 reduction in the 1–11 AU region seen after 1980. Moreover, it is difficult to find a physical reason to reduce gradient drift throughout the solar cavity even for the observed field turbulence (Isenberg and Jokipii 1979; Moussas et al. 1982). The greater depth of modulation observed for electrons during the $A = +1$ epoch (Garcia-Munoz et al. 1987) is predicted by the tilted neutral sheet model (Potgieter and LeRoux 1987a), although there are still discrepancies when the exact tilt-intensity relation is considered. Recently, Jokipii and Kota (1989) have suggested that drift is not important within about 10% of the solar poles beyond about 5 AU, because solar-generated transverse waves grow relatively more quickly when compared with the mean Parker field in this region. The result is that diffusion is enhanced along the polar field lines, the radial gradient here becomes greater, and finally the equatorial gradients at A = +1 which are controlled entirely by pole to equator flow in turn increase. Kota (1989) numerically demonstrates that equatorial radial gradients during the A = +1 and A = -1 epochs could be found which are within a factor of 2 of each other. We will, therefore, adopt a two-stage modulation model whereby a barrier acts for A = +1 in series with an axisymmetric, tilted neutral sheet model with drift velocities effectively reduced, but still sufficient to give noticeable intensity changes. # III. OUTLINE OF BARRIER MODEL The interaction of the solar wind flow and the interstellar medium motion relative to the Sun may produce two shock fronts, as shown in Figure 2 taken from Baranov et al. (1970), where the axis of symmetry is the line parallel to the interstellar motion intersecting the Sun. An approximation to the overall topography is the model of Parker (1961, 1963) shown in Figure 3, who assumed subsonic flow within the stellar medium, and, indeed, the presence or absence of an outer shock is not crucial to our arguments. Between the termination ![](images/24b883272bbc755249c2178892431d4f59007656350bdad73e1655264ec64f72.jpg)  
