FIG. 3.—Parker (1961, 1963) model of the overall topography of the interaction region between the interstellar medium and the heliosphere. (Axford 1972). The axis of symmetry is thought to lie at $8^{\circ}$ above the solar equatorial plane. Entry of cosmic rays through the barrier between the contact discontinuity and the inner shock is inhibited due to the competition of the solar wind sweeping and the $(K_{rr} \cdot \partial U/\partial r)$ diffusive flux. The quantity $K_{rr}$ (proportional to $K_{\parallel} \cos^{2}\Psi$ ) is likely to be larger in the equatorial plane for two reasons. First, the average Parker spiral magnetic field convected through the shock is at a larger angle to the radius vector than in the solar polar regions. Second, merged interaction regions, isolated shocks, etc., associated with solar activity are more likely at lower heliolatitudes—these plasma disturbances will bring a higher level of magnetic turbulence to the inner termination shock, which in turn will probably result in an increase in level of postshock turbulence over that which occurs at high heliolatitudes. Note that in contrast to the supposed saturation of the magnetic wave growth over the poles, there is greater energy in the combined plasma kinetic and magnetic energies associated with solar activity at lower latitudes. By analogy with the solar wind, $K_{\perp}/K_{\parallel} < 1$ and cross-field diffusion to short-circuit the radial convection is difficult, and, moreover, there is an extra convective sweeping opposing motion parallel to the boundary. Formally, the solution of the complete modulation problem is a double integration of $$
\boldsymbol {\nabla} \cdot (\bar {\boldsymbol {V}} U - \boldsymbol {K} \cdot \boldsymbol {\nabla} U) - \frac {1}{3} \boldsymbol {\nabla} \cdot \bar {\boldsymbol {V}} \frac {\partial}{\partial T} (\alpha T U) = 0, \tag {3}
$$ over the complete heliosphere to the outer shock. Since this is a formidable undertaking, involving a solution for V within the boundary, we indicate an approximate treatment. Within the inner shock, the streaming results, and the lack of drift dominance requires $|S| < |CVU|$ and we make the same assumption within the boundary layer. Referring to Figure 4, where we indicate schematically the modulation barrier and cosmic-ray flow patterns, we see that $|S| = CVU - K\nabla U$ is satisfied by $$
U _ {\mathrm{I}} = U _ {s} \exp \left[ \left(- \int_ {r} ^ {r _ {s}} \frac {C V}{K _ {r r}} d r\right) \right], \quad r <   r _ {s}, \tag {4}
$$ and, because $\nabla \cdot V = 0$ at $r > r_{s}$ , the solution of equation (3) is $$
U _ {\mathrm{II}} = U _ {o} \exp \left[ \left(- \int_ {r _ {s}} ^ {r _ {o}} \frac {V}{K _ {r r}} d r\right) \right], \quad r _ {s} <   r <   r _ {o}, \tag {5}
$$ where $r_{s}$ is the boundary of the inner shock and $r_{o}$ is the contact discontinuity radius beyond which modulation cannot occur. Spherical symmetry and continuity of U at $r = r_{s}$ are assumed. With equation (5) for U in region II (see Fig. 4)

